Topology Response: do not wait for, or report BSSs from, a disabled radio.

An agent answers a 1905 Topology Query only after checking that every em_t
belonging to the queried AL MAC has reached em_state_agent_onewifi_bssconfig_ind:

    for (auto &em : em_radios) {
        if ((em->get_service_type() == em_service_type_agent) &&
            (em->get_state() < em_state_agent_onewifi_bssconfig_ind)) {
            em_printfout("radio %s is not configured, ignoring", ...);
            em_radios.clear();
            return;
        }
    }

A radio that is disabled never runs through OneWifi's VAP configuration, so its
em_t stays at em_state_agent_unconfigured for the lifetime of the process. The
loop above therefore returns on every single Topology Query, forever, and the
agent never sends a Topology Response at all -- not even for the radios that are
perfectly well configured.

Observed on both agents in this container setup, where the 6GHz radio is
disabled (see ccsp-one-wifi patch 0006):

    ctrl:  40 Topology Queries sent to 02:00:00:7d:10:c0, one per second
    agent: 40 x "radio 02:00:00:7d:10:c0 is not configured, ignoring"
           0  x "All radios are configured ... sending topology response"

and on the extender:

    41 x "radio 02:00:00:d5:d9:bc is not configured, ignoring"

02:00:00:d5:d9:bc is Band 3 (6GHz), vap private_ssid_6g, radio index 2, whose
data model entry reads "RadioName": "radio3", "Enabled": false, "FreqBand": 16
in the OneWifi JSON the agent ingests. The 2.4GHz and 5GHz radios in the same
JSON are "Enabled": true and do reach bssconfig_ind.

The consequence is not limited to topology reporting. The AP Operational BSS TLV
rides in send_topology_response_msg(), so while this loop keeps returning early
the controller never learns any agent's operational BSSs: BSSList comes back
empty for every radio, no station is ever attributed to a BSS, and steer_sta
answers Error_No_Config_Change_Detected. Client steering is unreachable until
the Topology Response is sent.

Skip radios whose data model says they are disabled instead of blocking on them.
This is the same test the dm layer already applies when picking a backhaul BSS
(dm_easy_mesh_t::get_backhaul_bss_info and its bsta counterpart both do
"if (!radio->m_radio_info.enabled || ...) continue;"), so a disabled radio is
already understood elsewhere to be something to step over rather than wait for.

Disabled radios are also excluded from the em_state_agent_topo_synchronized
assignment that follows a successful send -- they were never synchronized, and
claiming otherwise would misreport their state.

create_operational_bss_tlv_topology() needs no change: it already skips disabled
BSSes, so a disabled radio simply contributes an entry with bss_num = 0, which
is what the spec expects for a radio with no operational BSS.

Note, not fixed here: em_capability.cpp's handle_ap_radio_basic_cap does
"radio_info->enabled = true;" unconditionally for every radio an agent reports.
The AP Basic Capability TLV carries no enabled bit, so there is nothing to
derive a real value from at that point, and the controller's view of every radio
is enabled regardless of the truth. That is a latent defect -- it can make the
controller pick a disabled radio's BSS as a backhaul candidate via the dm checks
mentioned above -- but it is on the controller side and is not what deadlocks
the Topology Response. Left alone rather than papered over with an invented
value.
Second hunk, found by testing the first one: with the Topology Response finally
being sent, the controller received it and still stored nothing --

    handle_topology_response:2243: Failed to handle operational BSS due to
    SSID misconfiguration.

repeated once per response, and BSSList stayed at 0 rows. The cause is the same
disabled radio. Its VAPs never received a WSC M2 (that is what configuring a
radio does), so they still carry the image default SSID, and the agent was
reporting them in the AP Operational BSS TLV:

    print_ap_operational_bss_tlv: Radio: 02:00:00:a6:3f:ee
    print_ap_operational_bss_tlv:   BSS:02:00:00:a6:3f:ee SSID:BPI-RDKB-MLO-AP-
    print_ap_operational_bss_tlv:   BSS:02:00:00:17:0d:e3 SSID:mesh_backhaul
    print_ap_operational_bss_tlv:   BSS:02:00:00:40:54:ab SSID:BPI-RDKB-MLO-AP-

The controller's handle_ap_operational_bss() checks every reported SSID against
NetworkSSIDList (private_ssid, iot_ssid, lnf_radius, mesh_backhaul, hotspot) and
returns -2 on the first one that does not match -- aborting the entire TLV, so
none of the BSSs from any radio are stored, not just the offending ones. The
controller logged exactly one distinct offender, 30 times: BPI-RDKB-MLO-AP-.

So report no BSSs for a disabled radio. A radio that cannot beacon has no
operational BSS by definition, which is what the TLV is for; the radio itself
is still listed, with bss_num = 0.

Worth noting separately: the controller discarding a whole TLV because one BSS
carries an unexpected SSID is fragile -- a single stale VAP anywhere in the mesh
suppresses topology for every radio on that agent. Skipping the offending BSS
would be more robust. Not changed here, because that validation drives the
ssid_mismatch/renew flow upstream deliberately, and fixing the agent to stop
reporting non-operational BSSs addresses the actual defect rather than making
the controller tolerant of bad input.

Upstream: unified-wifi-mesh v0.3.1 (SRCREV c0e72a3).

